---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DemoCard from '../components/DemoCard.astro';
import { demos } from '../data/demos';
---

<Base
  title="Demos — fibtransponder"
  description="Six interactive demos, each calling this repository's Go compiled to WebAssembly. No JavaScript re-implementations, no recorded screenshots."
  kicker="Interactive"
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Six demos, no pre-baked data</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      Every figure on this site is produced by <strong>this repository's own Go code</strong>,
      compiled to WebAssembly at build time. There is no JavaScript re-implementation of any
      algorithm and no recorded JSON fixture anywhere in the project.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      That has a cost — a 1 MB brotli download, paid only on these pages — and a benefit: a number
      cannot go stale, because there is nothing stored to go stale. The same golden values that
      <code>make ci</code> asserts on in Go are what the page renders.
    </p>
  </header>

  <ul class="demo-grid">
    {demos.map((d) => <DemoCard demo={d} />)}
  </ul>

  <section class="how panel framed measure">
    <h2>How the plumbing works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <code>make wasm</code> compiles <code>internal/wasmapi</code> — a thin JSON transport over
        the internal packages, with no <code>syscall/js</code> in it — for
        <code>GOOS=js GOARCH=wasm</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The site loads it lazily and calls one function,
        <code>fibtransponder.call(method, argsJSON)</code>, which returns a JSON envelope.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every <code>uint64</code> crosses as a <strong>decimal string</strong>, never a JSON
        number. JavaScript numbers are float64 and lose integer precision above 2⁵³, which every
        sketch here exceeds; a round-trip test in Go pins this.
      </li>
      <li>
        A failure comes back as a coded envelope (<code>input_too_large</code>,
        <code>bad_args</code>, <code>unsupported_method</code>) rather than a panic, so a bad
        input can never take the page down.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The shim is <a href="https://github.com/shaoyanji/fibtransponder/tree/master/internal/wasmapi">internal/wasmapi</a>,
      and its tests are part of ordinary <code>make ci</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head {
    margin-bottom: 2.25rem;
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }
  .page-head p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }

  .demo-grid {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.15rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 21rem), 1fr));
  }

  .how {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding: 1.75rem 1.9rem;
  }
  .how h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .how ol {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    font-size: 0.93rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .how li + li {
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
  }
  .how p {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
